Recently I'm considering playing nethack again after a long break.  On
checking for new versions of the patches I use, I noticed some notes on
Bilious appended to a patch I myself made, "use_darkgray".

These referred to some mysterious problem with "white glyphs", and
linked to a fixed version.  I compared this version with my own release,
and found just one difference -- their version adds an explicit
"set-foreground-color-7" terminal code when outputting CLR_WHITE glyphs.

That shouldn't be necessary, since nethack emits an SGR0 code after each
use of color and/or bold -- there should never be a need to explicitly
say white.

If this was to support a funny terminal that has some non-white color as
its default after SGR0, then it would also be necessary to set an
explicit color when outputting CLR_GRAY glyphs -- which as far as an
ANSI terminal goes, are just white without a bold attribute.  But the
modified patch does not do that.

(Also, most such "funny terminals" are those that use black on white
text.  On such a terminal, explicitly setting a white foreground would
be harmful.)

Could someone explain what is going on here?

Needless to say, *I* experience no problems with "white glyphs" using
the original version of my patch.


(The original version of my patch is available from:
ftp://talamasca.ocis.net/pub/michael/release/nethack-3.4.3-gray-1.diff.bz2

I'll probably release a "gray-2" if the comments here indicate there is
actually a problem.)

